{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788296586,"nonce":"W3FzmD9kWrGItEMm7BMrLEwf0Fg=","message":"FIRST BENCH CONTACT, AND THREE OF THE FIVE ACCEPTANCE BULLETS ARE NOW MEASURED. Session 21, orchestrator, bench `_RimWorld-Agent` session `20260901T205859`, `--quicktest`, game 1.6.4871 rev600, assembly `1.0.0+52606d1` (the artifact `db08b3d` carries). Raw envelopes banked at `accept/runs/s21-20260901/`, commit `a831742`. **The acceptance suite is still owed and is being written in parallel — this is the orchestrator proving the dig paths exist before a suite is built on them.**\n\nOne pawn, four reads.\n\n| read | hediffs_total | rows | dropped | BloodLoss row | severity | life_threatening | clock |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| healthy | 5 | 5 | 0 | — | 0 | — | `null` |\n| bleeding | 53 | 20 | 33 | **absent** | 0.007 | — | 16,317 |\n| + BloodLoss 0.478 | 54 | 20 | **34** | **0** | 0.562 | **false** | 7,223 |\n| healed | 5 | 5 | 0 | **0** | 0.623 | **true** | `null` |\n\n## The third read is this issue\n\nFifty-four hediffs against a cap of twenty. **Thirty-four dropped — a harder truncation than any of Captain's five M1 reads, whose worst dropped 19 — and `BloodLoss` is row 0.** Under the sort that shipped before session 20 it was rank 0, below `TendableNow` and below `Hediff_MissingPart`, and it was cut from all five of his reads. `hediffs_more` reports 34 honestly.\n\n## The Scope's own alternative is now refuted by measurement, not by argument\n\nComment #1 argued from the def XML that \"a severity/lethality ordering that puts life-threatening hediffs first\" would have reproduced the bug, because `lifeThreatening` sits on `BloodLoss`'s fifth stage at `minSeverity 0.60` and Captain died at ~0.478. **That is now a reading rather than an inference.** On the third read, at severity 0.562, that row's `life_threatening` is **false**. One read later, past 0.60, the same field on the same pawn is **true**. The alternative would have cut the row at exactly the severity band where it mattered and surfaced it only once the pawn was already in the extreme stage.\n\n## The clock's independence from the hediff list, demonstrated\n\nSecond read: `BloodLoss` is **absent from the rows** at severity 0.007 — legitimately, since stage 0 sets `becomeVisible false` and `Hediff.Visible` is the player's own filter — while `ticks_until_bleedout` reads **16,317** anyway. That is the decision recorded in DESIGN, shown rather than asserted.\n\n## Both directions on ONE pawn\n\n`ticks_until_bleedout` is **present-and-`null`** for a non-bleeder (the key exists, so a suite's existence assertion is meaningful) and finite for a bleeder, proven by transitioning the same pawn rather than by comparing two. `game_shows_clock` tracks it: false / true / true / false.\n\n## What this does NOT prove, said rather than skipped\n\nThe suite itself. The in-game readout comparison (that is a human reading the health tab and is on the orchestrator's list). `work-cover`, `triage`, and `triage.act` executing as a real `rescue`. Those are `40ed42f`.\n\n## Two findings for whoever writes the suite, both measured\n\n- **`dev:heal` does not clear `BloodLoss`.** It is `HealthUtility.HealNonPermanentInjuriesAndRestoreLegs`, and `BloodLoss` is not a `Hediff_Injury`. The fourth read has every injury gone, five hediffs left, and `blood_loss_severity` **higher than before the heal** (0.623 vs 0.562) — because with the bleeding stopped it is still the severity it accumulated to. A fixture that heals between phases does not get a clean subject.\n- **`dev:damage {mode:\"amount\"}` stops at Downed by construction**, so the Captain shape needs a small `amount` and repeated calls, not one call with `hits:20`. Three calls of `{hits:8, amount:2}` reached 53 hediffs on a standing pawn.\n\n## One shape question, raised and not decided from the chair\n\n`bleedout.outcome` is `\"death\"` on both reads where `ticks` is `null`. It is defensible — DESIGN says `outcome` answers the hypothetical \"what happens WHEN the clock runs out\", which has an answer whether or not a clock is running — but it is not obviously right for a pawn who has no clock, and any suite asserting `outcome` has to know it is populated regardless. Left to the worker writing the suite to rule on and record, rather than changed here.","files":null}]}